Formal-001
ChipVerify Z3: "Log stream connection lost." Yosys base, prep and SMT2
generation returned 0. Z3 started, then the displayed log ended.
Suggested fix: Persist the worker log and exit status by job ID. Reconnect
the viewer to the existing job and replay its log. Track stream status and
solver result independently so a disconnect cannot appear as a solver timeout.

Formal-002
The exact ChipVerify formal_props.sv assertion body was unavailable. The
repository also contains two RTL copies. The local run follows the synthesis
filelist, uses a repository-owned harness, and excludes the unused FPGA
wrapper and simulation testbench. Local results apply to these inputs.
Fix: Keep the harness under version control. Export its content, resolved
filelist, and input hashes with every job when comparing environments.

LOCAL PROPERTY SCOPE
One assumption requires reset at the first sampled rising edge. Later reset
values are unconstrained. The RTL supplies the fixed instruction ROM.
Seven assertions check PC alignment, PC and MMIO reset, the store's instruction
word and effective address, the resulting MMIO value, and MMIO stability
outside reset and the store. Two covers establish that the boot program
reaches the store at PC 0x0000000c and produces 0x00005555 at MMIO 0x00007000.
The bounded checks apply to the built-in boot program. ISA-wide coverage
requires separate properties and instruction stimulus.

LOCAL TOOLCHAIN
Yosys 0.66 (86f2ddebce7e98ce7cacc27e8a5c14cb53b51b51)
yosys-smtbmc driver: Yosys upstream e419c4eef77f953212f2dbc6af88d4eaef177084
Solvers: Bitwuzla 0.10.0, Z3 4.13.3, Boolector 3.2.4
The local runner calls yosys-smtbmc directly, the backend used by the
ChipVerify job. It builds one SMT2 model per memory representation and
runs each solver with the same depth, assumptions, and wall-clock budget.

Formal-003
Local setup errors:
  ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'smtio'
  boolector: error while loading shared libraries: libboolector.so
  Bitwuzla 0.10.0: invalid option '--smt2' (legacy driver), BrokenPipeError
  Python SyntaxWarning: invalid escape sequences in the Yosys 0.23 driver
Fix: Expose the Python support modules and shared libraries from the
extracted tool packages. Use a driver that invokes current Bitwuzla with
--lang smt2. Its incremental mode handles successive SMT-LIB commands.
The reported comparison uses the compatible driver for all three engines.

Formal-004
Early model export with Yosys 0.66 produced no assertion, assumption or
cover metadata until the clocked property cells were lowered.
Fix: Run async2sync for this single-clock model before SMT export. The
runner checks that the model contains each property type before solving.
The first mapped export also raised:
  ERROR: Unsupported cell type $sdffce ... please run dffunmap before write_smt2.
Fix: Run dffunmap after memory_map and opt -full to lower synchronous
reset/enable flip-flop forms for the SMT2 backend.

Formal-005
Performance finding: Preserving SMT arrays in this design creates large
write chains, including reset writes for every data memory word. With
mapped memories the SMT2 file is smaller and all engines finish the checks.
The array build plus Bitwuzla is faster for a single-solver boot run. Mapping
adds build time but gives a reliable three-solver baseline within this budget.
Fix: Use mapped memories by default for this RISC-V baseline. Keep the
array representation available via --memory-model arrays. Measure both
representations before choosing a default for other designs.
No DUT RTL changes were needed for the completed checks.

ENGINE BEHAVIOR AND VLSI SUITE 101
The BMC driver builds successive copies of the transition relation. At each
depth it searches for a trace that satisfies the assumptions and violates
an assertion. A satisfiable violation query yields a counterexample.
Covers use reachability queries to check that the boot scenarios can occur.
- Bitwuzla rewrites fixed-size bit-vector terms and translates them into
  SAT constraints. It can abstract expensive arithmetic and refine it when
  a candidate needs more exact reasoning. Use it first for fast feedback on this benchmark.
- Boolector rewrites bit-vector terms, bit-blasts them to SAT, and uses
  lemmas on demand for arrays. Keep it as a second engine for cross-checks.
- Z3 is a general-purpose SMT solver. Its standard bit-vector backend
  bit-blasts. Word size and the selected model representation can change
  the Boolean problem size, including when the RTL has no complex math.

Suite design: Reuse the model, retain per-property and per-depth timing,
and benchmark with pinned versions and equal budgets. For larger designs,
slice the model to the state and logic each property depends on, and prove
ALU, decode, and memory behavior with focused harnesses. Keep integrated
boot properties and reachability checks for the composed design. Use
assertion mutation in regression to verify that the flow emits a trace.
Track timeout, cover unreachable, inconsistent assumptions, counterexample,
and tool error as distinct outcomes. Compare solver portfolios across
representative designs before making suite-wide defaults.

References:
  https://bitwuzla.github.io/data/smtcomp2025/paper.pdf
  https://boolector.github.io/papers/BrummayerBiere-TACAS09.pdf
  https://microsoft.github.io/z3guide/programming/Parameters/
  https://symbiyosys.readthedocs.io/en/latest/verilog.html

REPRODUCE FROM REPOSITORY ROOT (LINUX)
Use a Yosys and yosys-smtbmc installation compatible with the three solver
versions, with all executables on PATH.
  python3 Formal/run_formal.py /tmp/riscv-formal-mapped --timeout 60
  python3 Formal/run_formal.py /tmp/riscv-formal-arrays --timeout 60 --memory-model arrays
Choose fresh output directories. Each run saves yosys.log, individual
solver logs, summary.json, and VCD traces when generated.

